# SEO and AI visibility

Every public page ships with search and AI discovery built in, not bolted on later. The goal: correct indexing, rich results where earned, and clear entity signals so humans and models can find, understand, and cite Fajita accurately.

Align with `company.mdc` for legal name and address. Align with `voice-and-boundaries.mdc` and `draper-honeycopy.mdc` for copy quality (specific copy ranks and gets cited; slop does not).

## Scope

| Surface | SEO treatment |
| --- | --- |
| Marketing, landing, pricing, docs, blog, legal | Full SEO + AI visibility |
| Auth-gated app (dashboard, workspace) | `noindex` unless a page is intentionally public |
| API routes | Not indexed |
| Preview/staging | `noindex` + `robots.txt` disallow |

When adding a route, decide index intent before shipping.

## Auto-implementation (no separate prompt)

Agents must implement SEO infrastructure **automatically** as part of frontend work. The user should never need to say "implement SEO," "add sitemap," or "set up llms.txt."

**On session start or before any public page work**, check whether the SEO foundation exists. If anything is missing, **create it in the same work session** before or alongside the requested page. Do not defer to a follow-up task.

### SEO foundation (ship once, maintain always)

| Asset | Location (Next.js App Router) |
| --- | --- |
| `metadataBase` + default metadata | `src/app/layout.tsx` |
| Organization + WebSite JSON-LD | root layout or home page |
| `robots.txt` | `src/app/robots.ts` or `public/robots.txt` |
| `sitemap.xml` | `src/app/sitemap.ts` |
| `llms.txt` | `src/app/llms.txt/route.ts` or `public/llms.txt` |
| OG defaults | `opengraph-image` or metadata `openGraph.images` |

Use real Fajita copy from `company.mdc`. Replace placeholder metadata (e.g. `title: "fajita.io"`) on first touch.

### Per-page auto-implementation

When creating or editing any public route, **in the same PR/commit**:

- Add or update `metadata` / `generateMetadata` for that route
- Add route to sitemap
- Update `llms.txt` if URLs or positioning changed
- Add JSON-LD when page type warrants it
- Verify index intent (`index` vs `noindex`)

Do not ship a new public page without its SEO package.

## Non-negotiables (every public page)

- One clear `<h1>` matching page intent
- Unique `<title>` (50–60 chars target) and meta description (140–160 chars target)
- Canonical URL (`rel="canonical"`)
- `lang="en"` on `<html>` (adjust if localized later)
- Semantic HTML (`main`, `nav`, `article`, `section`, headings in order)
- Meaningful text in HTML on first load (no empty shell requiring JS for core meaning)
- Descriptive alt text on informative images; empty alt on decorative
- Internal links with descriptive anchor text (not "click here")
- Mobile-friendly layout and tap targets
- Fast LCP: optimize hero images, fonts, and blocking scripts
- No placeholder meta ("fajita.io", "Coming soon" as sole description)

## Next.js App Router requirements

Use the Metadata API (`export const metadata` or `generateMetadata`) per route. Do not rely on client-only title updates for public pages.

Per public route provide where applicable:

```text
title
description
alternates.canonical
openGraph (title, description, url, siteName, images, type)
twitter (card, title, description, images)
robots (only when diverging from default index/follow)
```

- Default site metadata in root `layout.tsx`; override per page
- Use `metadataBase` with production URL once domain is known
- Prefer static or SSG/ISR for indexable marketing content
- `generateStaticParams` for programmatic SEO pages (docs, comparisons) when added

## Required root files

Ship and maintain at production domain root:

| File | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `/robots.txt` | Allow/disallow paths; reference sitemap |
| `/sitemap.xml` | All indexable public URLs with `lastmod` |
| `/llms.txt` | AI-oriented site summary, key URLs, usage guidance |
| `/favicon.ico` | Brand mark in SERPs and tabs |
| `opengraph-image` or explicit OG images | Share and citation previews |

Keep `robots.txt`, `llms.txt`, and sitemap reachable (see `src/middleware.ts` matcher).

### robots.txt baseline

- Allow public marketing, docs, legal
- Disallow `/api/`, authenticated app paths, staging utilities
- Include `Sitemap: https://[production-domain]/sitemap.xml`

### llms.txt baseline

Plain, factual, quotable. Include:

- What Fajita is (one paragraph)
- Who it is for
- Primary URLs (home, pricing, docs, signup)
- Company name and contact per `company.mdc`
- Preferred name for the product
- Link to full policy pages (privacy, terms)
- Optional: `llms-full.txt` with expanded product detail for models that support it

Update `llms.txt` when positioning, pricing, or core features change.

## Structured data (JSON-LD)

Add valid JSON-LD on relevant public pages. Minimum site-wide:

- **Organization** (name: Fajita, url, address from `company.mdc`, logo when available)
- **WebSite** (name, url, `potentialAction` SearchAction if site search exists)

Add when content exists:

- **SoftwareApplication** or **Product** on product/pricing pages
- **FAQPage** on pages with real FAQs
- **Article** / **BlogPosting** on blog posts
- **BreadcrumbList** on nested pages
- **WebPage** for generic marketing pages

Rules:

- JSON-LD must match visible on-page content (no false claims)
- No fake `aggregateRating` or `review` without real verified data
- Validate with Google Rich Results Test before ship

## Content standards (ranking and citations)

AI systems and search engines reward **specific, true, well-structured** content.

**Do:**
- Lead with what the product does in plain language
- Use descriptive H2/H3 sections (problem, how it works, proof, pricing, FAQ)
- Answer real questions users ask (good for FAQ schema and AI answers)
- Keep claims verifiable; link to sources where appropriate for a citation product
- Refresh titles and descriptions when positioning changes

**Do not:**
- Keyword stuffing or hidden text
- Duplicate thin pages for long-tail spam
- Empty SaaS filler (see `voice-and-boundaries.mdc`)
- `noindex` on important landing pages by accident
- Block all bots in `robots.txt` unless legal requires it

## AI crawler visibility

This project tracks AI crawler traffic via `@datafast/ai-crawl` in `src/middleware.ts`. Preserve that integration.

- Do not break middleware reachability for indexable paths
- Prefer allowing major search and answer-engine crawlers unless policy dictates otherwise
- Monitor crawler logs after launches and route changes
- When adding auth middleware, exclude `robots.txt`, `sitemap.xml`, `llms.txt`, and public static assets from redirects

Entity clarity helps models recommend correctly:

- Consistent product name (**Fajita**) across title, H1, `llms.txt`, and Organization schema
- Visible pricing and feature truth on indexable pages
- About or company context with address per `company.mdc`

## Performance and Core Web Vitals

SEO and UX share metrics. Meet `frontend-quality.mdc` and:

- Set width/height on images; use modern formats (WebP/AVIF)
- Limit render-blocking scripts on marketing pages
- Font subsetting and `font-display: swap`
- Target good LCP, INP, and CLS on mobile

## Internal linking and IA

- Homepage links to primary conversion and proof pages
- Footer: legal, contact, docs, pricing
- Docs and blog link back to product pages with descriptive anchors
- Avoid orphan pages (no inbound internal links)

## App vs. marketing

The authenticated app is usually **not** a growth surface for organic search:

- Default app layout: `robots: { index: false, follow: false }` unless page is public by design
- Public share links (if any): intentional metadata and canonical per share URL
- Do not leak private user content into sitemap or structured data

## Checklist before shipping a public page

- [ ] Unique title and meta description
- [ ] Canonical URL set
- [ ] One H1; logical heading order
- [ ] OG + Twitter images
- [ ] Added to `sitemap.xml`
- [ ] JSON-LD if applicable and validated
- [ ] `llms.txt` updated if positioning or key URLs changed
- [ ] Core content visible without JS
- [ ] Mobile and performance spot check
- [ ] No accidental `noindex`
- [ ] Company entity consistent with `company.mdc`

## Checklist before launch

- [ ] Production `metadataBase` URL configured
- [ ] `robots.txt`, `sitemap.xml`, `llms.txt` live
- [ ] Organization + WebSite JSON-LD on home or layout
- [ ] Google Search Console property verified (document in internal runbook, not customer-facing)
- [ ] Submit sitemap after first deploy
- [ ] Spot-check rendered HTML (View Source), not only React tree
- [ ] AI crawler tracking still firing in middleware

## When to update this work

- New public route or section
- Positioning, pricing, or product name change
- Domain or URL structure change
- Major redesign (preserve or improve metadata and schema)
- Adding docs, blog, or programmatic pages

